This report is about the findings of a study to explore the views of people with dementia and their carers about validation therapy and reality orientation.

\section{What is validation therapy?}
Validation therapy is a way of communicating with people with dementia. It is based on the idea that people with dementia are still in touch with their feelings and can understand what is being said to them. Validation therapy involves listening to what the person is saying and responding to what they are feeling. In this way, the person who has dementia can feel understood and more at ease.

\section{What is reality orientation?}
Reality orientation is a way of helping people with dementia to know where they are and what time it is. It involves telling the person where they are, what time it is and what the weather is like. It can also involve showing the person where they are by pointing out landmarks or using a map.

